{"duration_api_ms":61966,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"09d2d27a-faa9-44e2-bc47-c1e085091c80","total_cost_usd":0.11809159999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13035,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"output_tokens":6504,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4323},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13035,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6504,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13035,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13035},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":6504,"cacheReadInputTokens":4538,"cacheCreationInputTokens":13035,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.11809159999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":4323,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# BIP: 39\\n\\n## Abstract\\nThis BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\\n\\nIt consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed. This seed can be later used to generate deterministic wallets using BIP-0032 or similar methods.\\n\\n## Copyright\\nThis BIP is licensed under the BSD 2-clause license.\\n\\n## Motivation\\nA mnemonic code or sentence is superior for human interaction compared to the handling of raw binary or hexadecimal representations of a wallet seed. The sentence could be written on paper or spoken over the telephone.\\n\\nThis guide is meant to be a way to transport computer-generated determinism with a human-readable transcription. It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\\n\\n## Generating the Mnemonic\\nThe mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits. With more entropy security is improved but the sentence length increases. We refer to the initial entropy length as ENT. The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\\n\\nFirst, an initial entropy of ENT bits is generated. A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash. This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy. Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist. Finally, we convert these numbers into words and use the joined words as a mnemonic sentence.\\n\\nThe following table describes the relation between the initial entropy length (ENT), the checksum length (CS), and the length of the generated mnemonic sentence (MS) in words.\\n\\n```\\nCS = ENT / 32\\nMS = (ENT + CS) / 11\\n\\n|  ENT  | CS | ENT+CS |  MS  |\\n+-------+----+--------+------+\\n|  128  |  4 |   132  |  12  |\\n|  160  |  5 |   165  |  15  |\\n|  192  |  6 |   198  |  18  |\\n|  224  |  7 |   231  |  21  |\\n|  256  |  8 |   264  |  24  |\\n```\\n\\nSource: https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0039.mediawiki\",\"decisions\":[],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"reason\":\"UTOXs and letters were factual/spelling errors for wallets and words\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 produces a binary seed, not an ASCII seed\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This BIP is licensed under the BSD 2-clause license.\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 is licensed under BSD 2-clause, not GPL\"},{\"segment\":\"a12\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"It's not a way to process user-created sentences (also known as brainwallets) into a wallet seed.\",\"reason\":\"süeed was a misspelling of seed\"},{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"## Generating the Mnemonic\",\"reason\":\"memonic was a misspelling of mnemonic\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The mnemonic must encode entropy in a multiple of 32 bits.\",\"reason\":\"demonic/23 bits corrected to mnemonic/32 bits per CS=ENT/32 in b11\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"The allowed size of ENT is 128-256 bits.\",\"reason\":\"table b12 gives ENT endpoints 128 and 256 in bits, not bytes\"},{\"segment\":\"b7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"reason\":\"SHA652 is not a real hash function; corrected to SHA256\"},{\"segment\":\"b8\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"reason\":\"checksum is appended to the end, not the start, of the entropy\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reconciled\",\"replacement\":\"Next, these concatenated bits are split into groups of 11 bits, each encoding a number from 0-2047, serving as an index into a wordlist.\",\"reason\":\"0-2047 requires 11 bits per group, matching MS formula in b11\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"This BIP describes the implementation of a mnemonic code or mnemonic sentence -- a group of easy to remember words -- for the generation of deterministic wallets.\",\"corrects\":\"a group of easy to remember letters -- for the generation of deterministic UTOXs\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 mnemonics encode words for wallets, not letters for UTXOs\"},{\"statement\":\"It consists of two parts: generating the mnemonic and converting it into a binary seed.\",\"corrects\":\"converting it into a ASCII seed\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 seed derivation output is binary, not ASCII\"},{\"statement\":\"This BIP is licensed under the BSD 2-clause license.\",\"corrects\":\"This BIP falls under the GPL License.\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"BIP-0039 specification (bitcoin/bips repository), Copyright section\",\"reason\":\"the published BIP39 text states the BSD 2-clause license\"},{\"statement\":\"A checksum is generated by taking the first ENT / 32 bits of its SHA256 hash.\",\"corrects\":\"SHA652 hash\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"SHA652 does not exist; BIP39 uses SHA256\"},{\"statement\":\"This checksum is appended to the end of the initial entropy.\",\"corrects\":\"appended to the start of the initial entropy\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"BIP39 appends the checksum after (at the end of) the entropy\"}]}","ttft_ms":46683,"type":"result","duration_ms":62043,"uuid":"d83ccf5f-fa07-4c35-beef-30396fdba75c","ttft_stream_ms":1323,"time_to_request_ms":125,"first_content_frame_ms":1323,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
